urn is THREE-valued, not boolean — the driver used to test it with `if (found)`, which is C-truthy on the negative VM give-up sentinels (`PCREC_ERR_STEPS`/`PCREC_ERR_FRAMES`, unprefixed since [ABI-NS]/D60) too, so a give-up took the match branch and printed `caps`, which the give-up path never writes (uninitialized stack reported as a confident match — verified live: the pre-fix driver against a tiny `--step-budget` artifact printed a fabricated `match` line with garbage spans). Now discriminates `found == 1`/`found == 0`/otherwise explicitly, printing `"steps\n"`/`"frames\n"` and exiting 3 (distinct from the normal exit 0 and the usage/malformed-input exit 2) on a give-up, so run.sh can — and now does — score it as its own HARD harness-level failure rather than comparing it against a `match`/`nomatch` expectation. Same bug shape as `tests/fuzz/fuzz_driver.c` (fixed, fuzzfix arc) and `src/gen/emit_dfa.c`'s `pcrec_emit_main` (K21, docs/dev/known_issues.md); `tests/registry/pc4_driver.c` still has the untouched `if (rx_search(...))` form as of this fix — a fourth, still-open site, flagged rather than silently patched here since PC-4 is a different subsystem outside this fix's scope. **[DD-14 wave A, 2026-08-24]:** the give-up print itself had the SAME bug shape one level down — it named exactly `PCREC_ERR_STEPS` and folded every OTHER code into `"frames"`, so a `PCREC_ERR_WORK` give-up (reachable via `tests/vm/`'s own driver, just never through this one before this fix) printed as a frame-capacity failure. Now an explicit chain names all four give-up codes (`steps`/`frames`/`work`/`recurse`; `recurse` is `PCREC_ERR_RECURSE`, reserved with no producer yet, D71 item 1) and an unrecognized negative return prints `"giveup %d\n"` rather than guessing. **[DD-14 wave A commit 2, 2026-08-24, D71 item 1]:** a FIFTH word, `"internal"` (`PCREC_ERR_INTERNAL`), joins the chain — it is BELOW the give-up floor and is NOT a give-up (the artifact caught its own analysis/emission inconsistency; module `lookaround`'s negative-polarity lookbehind end-check is the one producer today), but it still exits 3 like the four give-up codes, since this driver has no third outcome shape, and run.sh's `gu` directive refuses to let any corpus block EXPECT it. **[DD-14.FB] critic pass, 2026-08-25:** on any non-default route the driver ALSO cross-checks `<prefix>_match_in` and `<prefix>_match_caps_in` against their un-suffixed siblings on the same `rx_ctx`, exiting **4** (its own outcome, named by run.sh ahead of the `gu` branch so it applies to every case kind) on a disagreement. This exists because those two entries had **no behavioural coverage anywhere in the tree** — every cell, driver and measurement drove `<prefix>_search_in` alone, so three entries shipped and one was exercised. It is a CROSS-CHECK rather than a second answer: the `.rxt` `m`/`n` vocabulary means a SEARCH and the anchored entries answer a different question, so each is compared to its OWN sibling (§10.2's "its un-suffixed sibling in every respect") and needs no oracle. The permitted divergence is SYMMETRIC — when a buffer is supplied the two may differ if EITHER is a give-up, since the caller's capacity is not the stamped one — and getting that wrong is how the check introduced itself: the first version allowed only the downward direction and went RED on a correct build at the `1024,8192` cell, where the `_in` entry MATCHES what its sibling refuses, which is the whole feature. MEASURED non-vacuous: an emitter whose `<prefix>_match_in` ignores its descriptor takes framebuffer.rxt from 16/0 to 12/4. **[DD-14.FB], 2026-08-25:** takes an optional THIRD argument, the ENTRY ROUTE (`default`/absent, `null`, `<n>`, `<frames>,<trail>`) that run.sh's `frames-buffer=` directive supplies. It changes WHICH ENTRY answers and nothing else — same subject, same startpos, same printed protocol, same exit codes — which is what makes a route a control rather than a variant. Buffers are `malloc`'d (so `RX_BUFFER_ALIGN` holds by definition) and sized in BYTES from the artifact's own `RX_RESUME_FRAME_SIZE`/`RX_TRAIL_FRAME_SIZE`, i.e. through exactly the arithmetic spec §10.4 publishes those macros for; on an artifact whose sizes read 0 (every DFA artifact) the route degenerates to a NULL descriptor, which that engine ignores anyway. The two capacities are separately settable on purpose: sabotage row S180 (each capacity bound to the other's array) is a NO-OP under any equal pair, so a route that could only set them equal could not detect it. Dormant here today: nothing in the .rxt `flags`/`features` directive vocabulary can select `--engine=vm` or a tiny budget, so no corpus case reaches the give-up path — deliberately not exercised by a new .rxt case in this commit (see this file's `run.sh` entry for the follow-on `gu`/`engine`/`budget` directives)
