 - **[DD-13b.W23.3] THE W23 PRODUCTIONS REACH BOTH HARNESS LEGS**, and the split between them is the format's own: every W23 schema row reads `validated_by: pcrec`, so legs B and C RECOGNISE these lines and CONSUME their bodies while pcrec VALIDATES them (`format_design.md` §2.14's last paragraph, published per row and rendered into §2.24's D4 table). What the two legs owe is STRUCTURE, and both gained it in the same shape: an ATTACHMENT STACK (leg A's `RxtFrame` stack — one entry per open level, so a DEDENT must land EXACTLY on an open level, which is what makes a mis-indented line inside an `ext` body a local error naming its own line) plus S3's OPAQUE REGION as streaming state (leg A reads the whole file and scans forward for a region's end; these two read one line at a time, so the same extent rule — to the first CONTENT line at or left of the opener's indent, a BLANK, or a COMMENT — is expressed as "a region is open, and this line either belongs to it or ends it"). The region is deliberately NOT the stack's business: a region's lines may be RAGGED, which the stack's exact-indent rule would refuse and all three legs accept. New arms in both: `pattern-esc` (a SECOND BLOCK OPENER; leg B passes the still-encoded text through with `pcrec --pattern-esc` and **decodes nothing in bash** — a `printf %b` approximation would be a second escape vocabulary drifting from pcrec's by construction — while leg C decodes with the eight-escape table it already has for subjects, and both refuse `\x00` by name citing K9), `mc` (the find-all count, §3.1's protocol in C and in python, **never `re.finditer`**, which differs on an empty-preferring pattern), `@file:` subjects with independently optional `as <id>` / `sha256 <hex64>` (the digest checked by whatever READS the subject; the `as` binding a FUNCTIONAL DEPENDENCY and not a uniqueness key, so restating it is normal and only a CONFLICTING re-binding is refused, naming both lines), `under` (a COUNTED, LABELLED SKIP in both legs — scoring a second correct answer per convention needs pcrec's convention BY NAME, which the harness must not hold, and AR-3's failure mode is exactly a skip nobody counted), `tag`/`oracle` (recognised and value-checked), and `provenance`/`variant`/`ext` (sub-block openers whose bodies are consumed; inside an `ext` OPEN SUBTREE nothing is dispatched at all, so a body key colliding with `pattern`/`m`/`provenance` produces no extra block, no extra case and no record anywhere). A block carrying a `@file:` subject, an `mc` case or a `pattern-esc` line is **not eligible for `HARNESS_BATCH`** (nor, since 2026-09-26, is a var-bearing block — module `vars`' `${name}` grows the artifact's own `<prefix>_search` signature by a trailing `vars, nvars` pair `dispatch_gen.sh` was never taught, see its own entry) — `dispatch.c` reproduces the default driver invocation only, and a second escape/counting vocabulary inside `dispatch_gen.sh` is the drift this format keeps declining. Leg B's `rxt_escape` gained `\n` in the same change and it is a CORRECTNESS fix rather than a cosmetic one: a prose region's value is the first dumped field that can contain a newline, and an unescaped one breaks the TSV row's framing (leg A's `put_escaped` and leg C's `DUMP_CTRL` both escape it already). **ONE THING THE THREE LEGS DO NOT AGREE ON, stated rather than left to be found**: the dump's `pattern` column for a `pattern-esc` block is the DECODED text in leg A and the text AS WRITTEN in legs B and C, because leg B cannot decode; the population is ZERO at this pin and the `esc` column W23.4 adds is what settles it.
